    {
      "id": "hook",
      "type": "hook",
      "title": "Stop brute-forcing",
      "body": "Most array questions in placement rounds are one of a few **patterns** in disguise. Spot the pattern and an O(n²) brute force becomes a clean O(n) pass. Two of the most common: **two pointers** and **sliding window**.\n\nIn five minutes you’ll watch both, then write the line that makes a window slide."
    },
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this program print?",
      "code": "public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] a = {1, 3, 4, 6, 9}; //~ Sorted, which two pointers relies on.\n        int target = 13;\n        int l = 0, r = a.length - 1; //~ One pointer at each end.\n        while (l < r) {\n            int sum = a[l] + a[r];\n            if (sum == target) { //~ Hit the target: print the pair and stop.\n                System.out.println(a[l] + \" + \" + a[r]);\n                break;\n            }\n            if (sum < target) l++; //~ Too small: move the left pointer right, to a bigger number.\n            else r--; //~ Too big: move the right pointer left.\n        }\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "13",
          "why": "It prints the pair, not the sum."
        },
        {
          "text": "4 + 9",
          "why": "Right: 1 + 9 and 3 + 9 are too small, so the left pointer moves until 4 + 9 = 13."
        },
        {
          "text": "3 + 9",
          "why": "3 + 9 is 12, still too small, so the left pointer moves on."
        },
        {
          "text": "6 + 9",
          "why": "The loop stops at the first pair that hits 13, which is 4 + 9."
        }
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
